i'm bex caswell olsen and i am the  director of book conservation at the  northeast document conservation center  today i'm going to walk you through how  my team conserves 170 year old book  this item is a record book that was  created between 1850  and 1913 and it contains marriage  records for a town in massachusetts  everything this book is written out by  hand and these records haven't been  copied or digitized this is the only  place where this information  exists and this is really important  historical information  the cover is torn in the joints and so  the front and back covers are really at  risk at falling off  the sewing that holds the pages together  is broken and so there are some pages  that have already become loose  that puts them at risk of being lost or  damaged  and then the ink that was used to write  this  book is suffering from iron gall ink  corrosion which will eventually cause  the ink to fracture and the paper to  just be eaten away by the ink  [Music]  the pages of this book got dirty over  time lots of people handling it  but also heating your house with you  know fireplace or coal  produced a lot of soot and that ends up  transferring to book pages  the first step in the conservation  treatment is surface cleaning using a  dry cleaning technique  if you don't surface clean first  of course when you mix water and dirt  you get mud and you don't want that on  the pages of your bunk we use a soft  brush  to remove dirt from the gutter of the  book and then the pages are cleaned with  the latex  sponge this isn't a sponge that you  would use wet it's something that you  use  dry and it very gently removes surface  dirt without causing abrasions or  leaving behind any kind of residue  [Music]  next we just bind the book so that means  that we remove the cover and the spine  we are using a nice sharp knife  the cover and the spine are going to be  saved because we're going to reuse them  later on in the repair  [Music]  after the cover is removed we use a  methyl cellulose  poultice to clean any old adhesives from  the spine of the text block  those old adhesives are quite aged  they've hardened  they've darkened and so we want to  remove them before we proceed  with taking the book apart and washing  we're going to let that sit under a  piece of saran wrap for a few minutes  to sort of soften the adhesive and then  we can  scrape it off or just sort of gently  scrape it off with a metal spatula  after the spine lining and any old  adhesives have been removed  we can proceed with removing the sewing  and separating the gatherings of pages  from one another  old paper can be very fragile and so  each stitch of sewing needs to be  removed  gently to avoid causing damage  before the pages can come in contact  with moisture of any kind we want to  test the inks to make sure that they can  withstand that  and we also want to test the iron gall  ink for the presence of free iron irons  if it tests positive for that that means  the ink is corroding and we need to take  some extra steps to slow that process  down  the inks in this book did test positive  which means we're going to be treating  it later on i know that washing paper  sounds kind of scary but if it's done  properly it's very safe  each page is placed on top of a piece of  non-woven polyester fabric and that's  going to provide support for the paper  while it's in the bath  and then we spray it with a 50 50  solution of water and ethanol  this helps to relax the paper and open  up paper fibers  [Music]  after about 10 or 20 minutes the tray  is drained and you can see a lot of  discoloration will come out in the first  bath so the water will be quite yellow  we'll repeat that process and change the  water until the water runs clear  after washing is complete we'll do  another bath in a  calcium phytate solution to stabilize  the iron gall  inks then we can rinse them with  filtered water  and move on to the next step  [Music]  because any sizing in the paper was  removed during the washing process we  need to resize it  this helps to add strength to the paper  and also provide some additional  protection  for the inks we use a diluted gelatin  solution  which is applied to damp pages in a thin  even layer  the size pages are then placed onto  felts to air dry in our drying rack  and after drying we can proceed with  mending  [Music]  to mend the paper we use a paste made  from purified  wheat starch and we mix that with water  and cook it while stirring constantly  and this gives us a really nice strong  adhesive that can be reversed with water  and then to make the paste even smoother  it's thinned out with water and strained  through a sieve  tears are repaired with wheat paste and  handmade japanese paper  this paper is very thin but very strong  we also need to reinforce spine folds to  prepare any breaks or weak areas along  the spine fold before we proceed with  resewing  [Music]  once all the mending is completed the  text block is reassembled and then put  in a press overnight to flatten it  before we re-sew the book is the best  time to send it off to our digital  imaging department where it can be  digitized  after imaging the text block is then  re-sewn with linen thread  which is very very strong  [Music]  after the book is sewn the spine is  pasted up with wheat paste  and that's going to help the book hold  its shape  the spine of the book gets pushed into  sort of a rounded shape with the backing  hammer  and then we put it into a jawbacker  where we back it so it kind of gives it  a little bit more  of that curved shape that you're used to  seeing in a hardcover book  next we need to add a paper spine lining  directly to the spine of the book  this is going to help it hold its shape  and prevent the book from opening  too sharply after the binding is  complete  while the spine lining is dry we can go  ahead and prepare the covers and since  we're reusing the original covers in  this repair we need to lift material on  the outside of the board  then we can reattach the covers by  inserting the sewing supports  and new spine material under that lifted  [Music]  leather  once we've got all that dry we can go  ahead and work on the inside of the  cover  and then last but not least we can go  back and touch up any areas  that are glaringly white and do any  toning to kind of help the repair blend  as much as possible  and we'll look at the item in both  artificial  and natural light to get a really good  idea of whether or not that color is  [Music]  accurate  after conservation the book doesn't  necessarily look much different and  that's actually kind of the point  we want to preserve as much of the  original material as possible  and the goal isn't to make it look brand  new it's to make it functional stable  and something that can be handled safely  [Music]  you
 

